# Terminal commands — prefer sed; always full SSH

## sed first

When giving the user a **terminal command** to inspect or change a file, prefer an **exact copy-paste `sed` one-liner** when it fits — especially on the origin host over SSH.

- **Inspect:** `sed -n '10,25p' /path/to/file`
- **Replace (Mac/BSD sed):** `sed -i '' 's/old/new/' /path/to/file`
- **Replace (Linux remote):** `sed -i 's/old/new/' /path/to/file`
- Use **real paths** from this repo or runbooks — no placeholders.

If `sed` is awkward (multi-line blocks, JSON), use another exact runnable command — still no placeholders.

## SSH — always the full one-liner

When a step runs on a **remote host**, print **one copy-pasteable line** with key, user, IP, and quoted remote command. Never “SSH to the server” without the command.

**Template:**

```bash
ssh -i ~/.ssh/7-5-25kuroit joshfialkoff@104.168.38.162 'REMOTE_COMMAND'
```

- **Key:** `~/.ssh/7-5-25kuroit`
- **User:** `joshfialkoff` (`opencode` only when deploy docs/scripts require it)
- **IPs:** `104.168.38.162` (current production); `75.127.14.185` (Dify migration host)

**sed over SSH example:**

```bash
ssh -i ~/.ssh/7-5-25kuroit joshfialkoff@104.168.38.162 'sed -n "1,40p" /opt/assistedly/compose.yaml'
```

**Local (this Cursor workspace on your Mac):** `npm run dev`, `sed -i '' '…' components/Foo.js`, etc. — no SSH wrapper.

See also `ssh-connection-format.mdc` and `terminal-commands-no-placeholders.mdc`.
